WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.951
Let's make your first Polling Pops Round
together.

00:00:02.951 --> 00:00:04.751
A Round is a set of questions

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:06.738
you can run live, without asking

00:00:06.738 --> 00:00:08.724
participants to create accounts.

00:00:08.724 --> 00:00:12.487
First, sign in with your email and
accept the policies.

00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:15.902
On a hosted site, open the link
in your inbox.

00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:18.255
This recording uses the local development

00:00:18.255 --> 00:00:20.953
shortcut, which is not available in
production.

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:23.161
Once you're in, your workspace keeps

00:00:23.161 --> 00:00:25.063
everything you create together.

00:00:25.954 --> 00:00:28.494
Choose Create, then start blank.

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:30.261
You can also use a starter or

00:00:30.261 --> 00:00:33.855
import existing work, but let's keep this
first try simple.

00:00:33.855 --> 00:00:36.625
Give the Round a name you'll recognize
later.

00:00:36.625 --> 00:00:39.222
We'll use a short safety-training example,

00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:41.818
and begin with a multiple-choice question.

00:00:41.818 --> 00:00:45.491
Choose Create Round and write question to
open the builder.

00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:48.369
Here, the question map is on the

00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:50.562
left, your content is in the middle,

00:00:50.562 --> 00:00:52.389
and settings are on the right.

00:00:52.389 --> 00:00:55.052
Write a clear prompt, replace the answer

00:00:55.052 --> 00:00:57.582
labels, and mark the correct response.

00:00:57.582 --> 00:01:00.246
Our team completed twenty-four of thirty

00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:02.711
checks, so eighty percent is correct.

00:01:02.711 --> 00:01:05.430
Add a short explanation for the reveal.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.149
Before moving on, wait for Saved.

00:01:08.149 --> 00:01:10.894
That way, you know the latest changes
are stored.

00:01:11.854 --> 00:01:15.296
Preview lets you check what participants
will see.

00:01:15.296 --> 00:01:17.884
Read the whole question, try the reveal,

00:01:17.884 --> 00:01:21.765
and make sure the correct answer and
explanation make sense.

00:01:21.765 --> 00:01:24.136
Return to the editor, then publish.

00:01:24.136 --> 00:01:26.746
Publishing freezes a reviewed version for

00:01:26.746 --> 00:01:29.419
delivery; it doesn't start a live session.

00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:31.297
You can keep editing the draft later

00:01:31.297 --> 00:01:33.801
without changing a Round that's already
running.

00:01:34.734 --> 00:01:37.596
Now open the live setup for your
published Round.

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:40.123
Check the audience limit, scoring, and

00:01:40.123 --> 00:01:43.382
results visibility, then create the live
session.

00:01:43.382 --> 00:01:46.225
The lobby gives you a seven-digit code,

00:01:46.225 --> 00:01:48.704
a direct join link, and a QR code.

00:01:48.704 --> 00:01:51.732
Share the link or show the QR on a screen.

00:01:51.732 --> 00:01:54.537
For phone testing, the join address must

00:01:54.537 --> 00:01:56.501
be reachable from the phone.

00:01:56.501 --> 00:01:59.632
Localhost only points back to that device.

00:01:59.632 --> 00:02:01.516
The code in this video is only

00:02:01.516 --> 00:02:04.091
a demo; use the code from your own lobby.

00:02:05.034 --> 00:02:06.929
Let's switch to a participant.

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:09.380
Open the shared link, scan the QR,

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.904
or enter the code on the join page.

00:02:11.904 --> 00:02:14.594
With these education settings, Polling

00:02:14.594 --> 00:02:17.355
Pops gives us a friendly session alias.

00:02:17.355 --> 00:02:19.842
We choose an avatar and join.

00:02:19.842 --> 00:02:22.226
No participant sign-in is needed.

00:02:22.226 --> 00:02:24.657
The lobby waits for the facilitator, so

00:02:24.657 --> 00:02:28.334
everyone can get connected before the
first question opens.

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:31.575
The facilitator starts the Round.

00:02:31.575 --> 00:02:34.092
Participants see the complete question and

00:02:34.092 --> 00:02:36.188
answer labels on their own devices.

00:02:36.188 --> 00:02:38.688
Choose a response and submit it.

00:02:38.688 --> 00:02:41.580
Look for Answer received and saved, rather

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.714
than assuming a tap was enough.

00:02:43.714 --> 00:02:47.696
Back on the host screen, lock the
responses, then reveal.

00:02:47.696 --> 00:02:49.921
That separates collecting answers from

00:02:49.921 --> 00:02:52.146
discussing them, and gives you a clean

00:02:52.146 --> 00:02:53.843
moment to explain the result.

00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:57.332
Finish the Round and open its report.

00:02:57.332 --> 00:02:59.907
You can review participation and question

00:02:59.907 --> 00:03:02.796
results, then come back through Results
later.

00:03:02.796 --> 00:03:05.408
Our quick test had just one participant,

00:03:05.408 --> 00:03:08.802
so it isn't meaningful evidence about a
whole group.

00:03:08.802 --> 00:03:11.764
Your turn: create one question, join from

00:03:11.764 --> 00:03:15.232
a second browser or phone, and complete
a Round.

00:03:15.232 --> 00:03:17.392
For the recovery workflow and room

00:03:17.392 --> 00:03:20.503
conversation tools, continue with the user
guide.
